At the operating system level, I/O devices (disk drives, network sockets, and the terminal emulator!)
do not understand "text." They only understand raw, uninterpreted streams of 8-bit bytes.


In Java, InputStream and OutputStream are the base abstract classes representing this raw binary pipeline.
Invoking read() on and InputStream, is plucking one 8-bit byte off the wire (returning an int in range of [0-255]).

However we don't read bytes, we read characters.
In Java, a char is a 16-bit data type representing a UTF-16 code unit.
Reader and Writer are the abstract classes built for this level of abstraction.
They manipulate 16-bit characters, strings, and textual semantics.

The Bridge: Character Encoding
This is how we get from an 8-bit byte stream to a 16-bit character stream
Through Character Encoding (like UTF-8, ASCII, ISO-8859-1).
An encoding is a translation dictionary.
It dictates how many bytes constitute a single character and how those bytes map to a specific Unicode symbol.

When a user types a command into their OS terminal, they press physical keys.
The terminal emulator translates those keystrokes into a sequence of bytes
based on the terminal's configured character set
(often UTF-8 on modern Linux/macOS, but historically something else on Windows).
These bytes are pushed into the standard input stream of the Java process (System.in, which is an InputStream).

If the shell wants to understand a command like echo "hello", it cannot just look at raw bytes;
It needs strings to parse tokens, handle quotes, and execute logic.

To move from the raw InputStream to usable text in Java,
we use a software design pattern called the Decorator Pattern.
We take the raw byte stream and wrap it in layers of increasingly sophisticated objects.


Here is structural snippet of how a shell connects to the OS standard input.
// ------------------------------ //
// The Raw Source (Bytes)
InputStream rawInput = System.in;

// The Bridge (Bytes -> Characters)
InputStreamReader decoder = new InputStreamReader(rawInput, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

// The Buffer (Characters -> Lines/Strings)
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(decoder);

// The Action
String command = reader.readLine();
// ------------------------------ //

Layer 1: System.in (The Input Stream)
    Has methods like read() which returns an int (a single 8-bit byte).

Layer 2: InputStreamReader (The bridge)
    This class is the literal bridge between byte streams and character streams.
    In the constructor we explicitly pass the InputStream and the Charset (StandardCharset.UTF_8).
    The Flow if we call decoder.read():
        this class reaches down into System.in
        It reads a byte.
        It looks at the UTF_8 rules
        It returns a single 16-bit char containing the letter.

Layer 3: BufferedReader (The Optimizer & Line Reader)
    Even though InputStreamReader safely gives us characters, we wrap it again in a BufferedReader.
    This object maintains an internal char[] array in memory (usually 8192 characters in size).
    The Flow if we call reader.readLine():
        The BufferedReader rapidly pulls characters from InputStreamReader
        and stores them in its array until it detects a newline character (\n or \r\n).
        Once it sees the newline, it takes the chunk of characters from its array,
        packages them into a single String, and hands it back.
